\section{Method}\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Problem Formulation}\label{sec:formulation}

Given a language model $\mathcal{M}$ and input $x$, we sample $N$ structured outputs $\{y_1, \ldots, y_N\}$ at temperature $T > 0$ via constrained decoding \cite{willard2023guided}.
Each $y_i$ is an entity set (NER) or relation set (RE).
A confidence signal $c$ serves two purposes: (a) \textit{quality estimation}---predicting which instances the model handles reliably, and (b) \textit{sample selection}---identifying the best output among $N$ candidates.

A \textbf{per-sample signal} scores each $y_i$ independently, $c_{\text{ps}}: (x, y_i) \to \mathbb{R}$, enabling direct candidate ranking.
An \textbf{instance-level signal} aggregates over all $N$ samples, $c_{\text{il}}: (x, \{y_i\}_{i=1}^N) \to \mathbb{R}$, producing a single score per input.
This asymmetry---between predicting \textit{which inputs} are easy and selecting \textit{which output} is best---is central to the correlation-selection gap we investigate.
Notation is summarized in Appendix~\ref{app:notation}.

\subsection{Confidence Signals}\label{sec:signals}

We use \textit{confidence signal} as the umbrella term for all five measures.
Four are \textit{consistency signals}---computed from inter-sample agreement---spanning two families: \textit{structural} (Soft Jaccard [SJ], entity voting confidence [VC]) and \textit{surface} (exact match [EM], Fleiss' $\kappa$ [FK]).
The fifth, log-probability, is the sole \textit{model-internal} signal and the only one that scores individual samples rather than the instance as a whole.

\paragraph{Log-probability (model-internal, per-sample).}
The only per-sample signal.
For sample $y_i$ with tokens $t_1, \ldots, t_{|y_i|}$:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:logprob}
c_{\text{lp}}(y_i) = \frac{1}{|y_i|} \sum_{j=1}^{|y_i|} \log p_{\mathcal{M}}(t_j \mid t_{<j}, x)
\end{equation}

\paragraph{Exact match voting (surface, instance-level).}
The majority fraction---the proportion of samples producing the most common output:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:em}
c_{\text{em}}(x) = \frac{1}{N} \max_{y} \big|\{i : y_i = y\}\big|
\end{equation}

\paragraph{Fleiss' $\kappa$ (surface, instance-level).}
We adapt \citet{fleiss1971agreement} to entity sets: items are unique (text, type) tuples across all $N$ samples; each sample assigns binary present/absent labels, yielding a $|E_\cup| \times 2$ rating matrix:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:fk}
\kappa = \frac{\bar{P} - \bar{P}_e}{1 - \bar{P}_e}
\end{equation}
where $\bar{P}$ is mean observed pairwise agreement and $\bar{P}_e$ is expected agreement under random assignment.
While FK assumes independent raters, temperature-based sampling provides sufficient response diversity for the statistic to capture inter-sample agreement patterns.
Empirically, we measure excess agreement ($\bar{P} - \bar{P}_e$) across all configurations (SciERC NER/RE, CoNLL, WNUT-17; $N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$) and find values between $-$0.4\% and $-$4.0\%, indicating mild \textit{negative} dependence---samples from the same model tend to err on different items---which confirms that FK is not inflated by inter-sample dependence.
Unlike structural signals, FK relies on exact string matching and does not tolerate surface variations.

\paragraph{Soft Jaccard (structural, instance-level).}
Our primary structural signal extends Jaccard similarity \cite{jaccard1901distribution} to extracted entity/relation sets.
For samples $y_i, y_j$, we group extractions by type, compute max-weight bipartite matching via the Hungarian algorithm (maximizing total token-level IoU; zero-IoU pairs automatically receive zero weight), and define:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:sj}
\text{SJ}(y_i, y_j) = \frac{\sum_{m \in M} \text{sim}(a_m, b_m)}{\sum_{g} \max(|g_i|, |g_j|)}
\end{equation}
where $M$ is the matched set from the Hungarian algorithm (without minimum similarity threshold), $\text{sim}(a_m, b_m)$ is the token-level IoU (intersection of tokens divided by union of tokens) between matched entity spans $a_m$ and $b_m$, and $g_i, g_j$ index per-type extraction counts.
We use $\max(|g_i|, |g_j|)$ rather than $|g_i \cup g_j|$ as the normalizer to penalize length asymmetry between samples.
The instance-level signal averages over all pairs:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:sj_instance}
c_{\text{sj}}(x) = \frac{1}{\binom{N}{2}} \sum_{i<j} \text{SJ}(y_i, y_j)
\end{equation}

\paragraph{Entity voting confidence (structural, instance-level).}
For each entity $e$ in the union $E_\cup$ of all samples, we compute frequency $\text{freq}(e) = |\{i : e \in y_i\}| / N$.
The instance-level confidence is the mean frequency:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:voting}
c_{\text{vote}}(x) = \frac{1}{|E_\cup|} \sum_{e \in E_\cup} \text{freq}(e)
\end{equation}
When $E_\cup = \emptyset$ (no entity extracted by any sample), we set $c_{\text{vote}}(x) = 0$.\footnote{This occurs in $<$1\% of instances in our experiments (WNUT-17 only). Our implementation computes the mean entity-level match rate against a majority threshold ($>N/2$). An alternative without the threshold yields slightly different absolute values but identical qualitative conclusions.}

\subsection{Evaluation Framework}\label{sec:evaluation}

\paragraph{Quality estimation.}
We measure rank correlation (Spearman $\rho$) between the confidence signal and instance-level F1, and binary discrimination (AUROC) by classifying instances as above or below median F1.

\paragraph{Sample selection.}
We report best-of-$N$ F1: for each instance, the signal selects one sample.
Per-sample signals (logprob) directly rank candidates; instance-level signals select the sample closest to the consensus output (the consensus-proximity score for SJ is mathematically equivalent to leave-one-out SJ selection).
We compare against an \textit{oracle} (highest-F1 sample) and \textit{greedy} ($T{=}0$, $N{=}1$).

\paragraph{Complementarity.}
High rank correlation does not guarantee effective sample selection: if per-sample variance is low, even a perfect quality estimator cannot distinguish among near-identical candidates (\S\ref{sec:selection_gap}).

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

\paragraph{Datasets.}
We evaluate on three English IE benchmarks spanning a difficulty gradient.
\textbf{SciERC} \cite{luan2018scierc} contains 551 test instances annotated for joint NER (6 types) and RE (7 types).
\textbf{CoNLL-2003} \cite{tjongkimsang2003conll} provides 3{,}453 test sentences with 4 entity types, where models achieve ${\approx}$91\% micro F1.
\textbf{WNUT-17} \cite{derczynski2017wnut} consists of 1{,}287 social media instances with emerging entities, used to probe method boundaries (52\% zero F1).

\paragraph{Models and sampling.}
Both \textbf{Qwen3-8B} and \textbf{LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct} are fine-tuned with LoRA (rank$=$32, $\alpha{=}64$) for 5 epochs using LLaMA Factory \cite{zheng2024llamafactory} under identical training procedures, ensuring comparisons across the two model families (both at 8B scale) reflect model differences rather than training confounds.
We draw $N{=}8$ samples at $T{=}1.0$ (primary), with $N{=}16$ for scaling experiments.\footnote{$N{=}16$ results for CoNLL (Qwen) and SciERC (LLaMA) are in Table~\ref{tab:robustness} (Appendix~\ref{app:robustness}).}
Quality estimation uses Spearman $\rho$ and AUROC; downstream utility uses best-of-$N$ selection F1 with exact span matching (NER) and head-tail-label matching (RE).
Inference uses vLLM with constrained decoding \cite{willard2023guided}.

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Quality Estimation Results}
\label{sec:qe_results}

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Quality estimation across tasks and models (Spearman $\rho$ and AUROC). Best value per block and column in \textbf{bold}. SciERC evaluates joint NER (6 types) and RE (7 types, single benchmark---preliminary); CoNLL evaluates NER (4 types). $N{=}8$ results use seed${=}42$. $N{=}16$ results (LLaMA CoNLL only; 3-seed mean $\pm\sigma$) are shown to illustrate $N$-scaling; additional $N{=}16$ results appear in Table~\ref{tab:robustness} (Appendix~\ref{app:robustness}).}
\label{tab:quality_estimation}
\begin{tabular}{ll cc cc cc cc}
\toprule
& & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{SciERC NER}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{SciERC RE}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{CoNLL (Qwen)}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{CoNLL (LLaMA)}} \\
\cmidrule(lr){3-4} \cmidrule(lr){5-6} \cmidrule(lr){7-8} \cmidrule(lr){9-10}
Signal & Family & $\rho$ & AUC & $\rho$ & AUC & $\rho$ & AUC & $\rho$ & AUC \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{10}{l}{\textit{$N{=}8$}} \\
VC & Structural & \textbf{.379} & .684 & \textbf{.350} & .693 & .424 & .789 & .426 & .709 \\
Soft Jaccard  & Structural & .360 & .692 & .250 & .670 & .436 & .801 & \textbf{.431} & .704 \\
Fleiss' $\kappa$ & Surface & .267 & .629 & .275 & .609 & .400 & .774 & .428 & .703 \\
Exact Match  & Surface    & .295 & \textbf{.749} & .134 & \textbf{.706} & \textbf{.451} & \textbf{.816} & \textbf{.431} & .705 \\
Log-prob     & Model      & .205 & .578 & .266 & .600 & .316 & .753 & .307 & \textbf{.761} \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{10}{l}{\textit{$N{=}16$ (3-seed)}} \\
VC & Structural & & & & & & & .458${\pm}$.003 & \\
Soft Jaccard  & Structural & & & & & & & \textbf{.463${\pm}$.007} & \\
Fleiss' $\kappa$ & Surface & & & & & & & .455${\pm}$.006 & \\
Exact Match  & Surface    & & & & & & & .460${\pm}$.008 & \\
Log-prob     & Model      & & & & & & & .310${\pm}$.005 & \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_signal_heatmap}
\caption{Quality estimation heatmap (Spearman $\rho$) across five signals and four configurations. Left: full evaluation set; right: conditional set (non-zero F1 instances). On the full set, structural signals dominate on SciERC while all signals converge on CoNLL. On the conditional set, EM emerges as the strongest signal on both NER ($\rho{=}0.360$) and RE ($\rho{=}0.417$), while logprob RE collapses to near-zero ($\rho{=}0.011$).}
\label{fig:signal_heatmap}
\end{figure*}

Table~\ref{tab:quality_estimation} presents rank correlation ($\rho$) and AUROC for five confidence signals across tasks and models.

\paragraph{Difficulty-dependent signal complementarity.}
On SciERC NER (complex schema, 6 types), structural signals lead: VC achieves $\rho{=}0.379$ and SJ $\rho{=}0.360$, both outperforming surface ($\rho{\leq}0.295$) and logprob ($\rho{=}0.205$).
This advantage persists on RE (VC $\rho{=}0.350$), though all signals weaken due to the 40\% zero-F1 floor effect.
VC and SJ show moderate instance-level correlation (NER: $\rho{=}0.659$; RE: $\rho{=}0.605$), indicating information overlap but not redundancy; their differential behavior across difficulty levels reflects the complementary granularity of entity-level frequency vs.\ span-level set overlap.
While the best $\rho{=}0.38$ (VC) explains approximately 14\% of F1 variance, the best AUROC reaches 0.749 (EM), indicating strong binary discriminative power for the quality/failure classification most relevant to practitioners in quality-aware routing.\footnote{For scale context, semantic entropy \cite{kuhn2023semantic} achieves comparable AUROC on discrete-answer QA, though direct comparison is not meaningful due to fundamentally different output spaces and evaluation protocols.}
Pairwise bootstrap CIs confirm the SJ advantage over logprob ($\Delta\rho{=}{+}0.155$, CI excludes zero) but not over EM ($\Delta\rho{=}{+}0.065$, marginal under Bonferroni correction; Table~\ref{tab:bootstrap_signal_ci}, Appendix~\ref{app:bootstrap_signal}).

\paragraph{$\rho$--AUROC dissociation.}
Signal choice depends on the intended use: on NER, EM leads AUROC (0.749) despite ranking third in $\rho$ (0.295), preferred for binary quality triage; structural signals ($\rho{\geq}0.36$) are better for fine-grained ranking.
On CoNLL with LLaMA, logprob leads AUROC (0.761) yet trails in $\rho$ (0.307), confirming this pattern across datasets.

\paragraph{Signal rankings shift with task difficulty.}
On CoNLL (greedy F1${\approx}$0.91), EM leads ($\rho{=}0.451$, AUROC${=}0.816$), surpassing SJ and VC.
When most samples are near-correct, binary agreement captures remaining variance more efficiently than set-level similarity.
This crossover---EM${>}$SJ on easy tasks vs.\ SJ${>}$EM on hard tasks---demonstrates difficulty-dependent complementarity rather than a universal structural advantage, suggesting practitioners should match signal type to expected task difficulty.
Cross-model stability is strong: SJ achieves $\rho{=}0.436$ (Qwen) vs.\ $0.431$ (LLaMA) on CoNLL ($\Delta{=}0.005$, within 3-seed $\sigma{\approx}0.007$).
On WNUT-17, where 52\% of instances receive zero F1, full-set correlations are misleading: VC achieves $\rho{=}0.724$ (AUROC${=}0.913$), but this is entirely driven by the zero/non-zero boundary (conditional $\rho{=}0.038$, n.s.; Table~\ref{tab:wnut}).
Logprob exhibits strong \textit{negative} correlation ($\rho{=}{-}0.410$, AUROC${=}0.219$).
We hypothesize that this reversal reflects overconfidence on out-of-distribution inputs \citep{xiong2024llm}: the model assigns high log-probabilities to fluent but incorrect entity patterns (e.g., common noun phrases that resemble but do not match rare WNUT entity types), while producing lower scores for genuinely uncertain outputs where it correctly hedges.
On the conditional set, all signals collapse toward weak or non-significant correlations ($\rho{\leq}0.35$), with SJ achieving only $\rho{=}0.089$ ($p{=}0.020$) on the full set---suggesting a capability boundary beyond which consistency signals capture binary success/failure but not quality gradation.

\paragraph{Conditional filtering.}
Filtering to non-zero F1 instances reshuffles rankings (Figure~\ref{fig:signal_heatmap}, right).
EM emerges as the strongest conditional signal on both NER ($\rho{=}0.360$) and RE ($\rho{=}0.417$), while VC and FK drop substantially (VC NER: $0.379{\to}0.300$; FK NER: $0.267{\to}0.176$).
Logprob RE collapses to $\rho{=}0.011$, confirming its full-set correlation was driven by zero/non-zero separation.
This creates a natural complementarity: VC and SJ are preferred on the full set, while EM provides the most precise ranking within the non-trivial subset (see Appendix~\ref{app:additional} for details).

%% ============================================================
\subsection{The Correlation-Selection Gap}
\label{sec:selection_gap}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_selection_gap}
\caption{The correlation-selection gap on SciERC ($N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$). Left: NER; right: RE. Bars show best-of-$N$ selection F1; the line marks quality estimation $\rho$. Despite $\rho$ up to 0.38, no signal significantly outperforms greedy on either task ($p{=}0.39$ for NER logprob, the best signal; Table~\ref{tab:selection}). Dashed line indicates oracle upper bound.}
\label{fig:selection_gap}
\end{figure*}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Best-of-$N$ selection F1 on SciERC ($N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$). RE results are from a single benchmark (preliminary). Best non-oracle result per column in \textbf{bold}. EM selects the plurality output; despite a 42\% pairwise exact-match rate, EM selection underperforms greedy on both tasks.}
\label{tab:selection}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Method & NER F1 & RE F1 \\
\midrule
Oracle & 0.776 & 0.585 \\
\midrule
Log-prob           & \textbf{0.650} & 0.395 \\
Soft Jaccard       & 0.645 & 0.386 \\
Exact Match        & 0.636 & 0.372 \\
VC                 & 0.634 & 0.359 \\
\midrule
Greedy ($T{=}0$)   & 0.644 & \textbf{0.404} \\
Random             & 0.608 & 0.354 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Despite strong quality estimation, confidence signals yield limited gains when used for best-of-$N$ selection (Table~\ref{tab:selection}, Figure~\ref{fig:selection_gap}).
Four of five signals are instance-level aggregates that assign a single score per input regardless of which sample is evaluated; they cannot rank candidates by construction.
The non-trivial question is whether log-probability---the only per-sample signal---can exploit the oracle headroom.
We anchor this analysis on SciERC, where output diversity is substantial: only 11--18\% of instances produce identical outputs across all $N{=}8$ samples, with 39--43\% unique outputs per instance (mean 3.5 distinct outputs), per-sample F1 standard deviation of 0.11--0.14, and mean pairwise SJ of 0.703.

\paragraph{No significant improvement over greedy.}
We exclude 22 instances with empty gold annotations ($n{=}529$) and evaluate significance via instance-level paired bootstrap resampling ($B{=}10{,}000$).
On NER, the oracle (0.776) reveals 13.2~pp of headroom above greedy (0.644).
Logprob achieves the highest selection F1 (0.650, +0.6~pp), but this is not significant ($p{=}0.39$; 95\% CI: [$-$3.0, +4.2]~pp; Cohen's $d{=}0.038$).
On RE, the best signal (logprob, 0.395) falls below greedy (0.404, $-0.9$\,pp, $p{=}0.80$), while 18.1~pp of oracle headroom remains.
TOST equivalence testing confirms most signals are indistinguishable from greedy at ${\pm}2$--$3$~pp on NER; on RE, EM and VC fail equivalence even at ${\pm}5$~pp (Table~\ref{tab:tost}, Appendix~\ref{app:diversity}).
VC ($\rho{=}0.379$, the best quality estimator) selects to only 0.634 on NER---\textit{below} greedy.
The gap extends to CoNLL (ceiling effect, 57--88\% identical outputs) and WNUT-17 (capability boundary; Table~\ref{tab:cross_selection}).

\paragraph{Quality estimation $\neq$ output selection.}
Instance-level confidence signals predict \textit{which inputs} the model handles reliably, not \textit{which output} among $N$ candidates is best.
When per-sample variance is low relative to instance-level difficulty, even perfect quality estimation cannot discriminate among near-identical candidates.
This gap has direct consequences for test-time scaling in IE \cite{brown2024monkeys, snell2024scaling}: closing it likely requires per-sample signals such as trained verifiers \cite{cobbe2021verifiers}.

